<!--
    RE-friction round 10, challenge 6869d705aadb6eeafb399027 (x86-64 Windows PE
    `crackmes.exe`): `kuna decompile ... sub_14000b970` rendered the OpenSSL
    call `int EVP_DigestFinal_ex(EVP_MD_CTX *, unsigned char *, unsigned int *)`
    as `EVP_DigestFinal_ex(v3,v9,v7,a3,1)`, five arguments for a
    three-argument function, and the invented fourth is the reader's OWN
    incoming parameter, so it looks load-bearing.

    Root cause (P4, active-input trial scoring).  The call site loads RCX, RDX
    and R8, never writes R9, and leaves a `1` in the Win64 fifth-argument slot
    at [RSP+0x20] that belongs to the frame's own bookkeeping:

        mov  dword ptr [rsp+0x20], 1
        lea  r8, [rsp+0x48]
        mov  rdx, qword ptr [rdi]
        mov  rcx, rsi
        call qword ptr [0x140014568]     ; a size-0 IAT import

    checkInputTrialUse scores the stack slot ACTIVE, while R9 reaches fillinMap
    as an UNREFERENCED filler trial buildTrialMap synthesized for the group
    nothing wrote.  ParamListStandard::forceInactiveChain then closes the hole:
    it records the highest active trial, the stack slot, and marks every
    inactive trial below it active, so buildInputFromTrials materializes the
    Varnode the R9 trial never had by reading R9 at the call, which resolves to
    the caller's untouched incoming R9.

    The ABI forbids the shape that repair reconstructs.  A ParamListStandard
    model assigns slots in resource order and reaches the stack only past a full
    register file, so a Win64 call has a fifth argument at [RSP+0x20] only if it
    has a fourth in R9.  An argument register with no Varnode at all is the end
    of the argument list, not a hole in the middle of one, which is the
    conclusion upstream already draws one slot to the right, where an
    unreferenced STACK trial at a call site sets `seenchain`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring".  The P0 assertion
    `option stackarggap on|off` decides whether an unreferenced argument
    REGISTER may end a CALL SITE's argument list when its next slot is on the
    stack.  The function's own input recovery is untouched (that is
    `inputparamgap`'s territory), and a call whose register file IS fully
    written keeps its stack argument.

    The fixture is the same construct, hand assembled: `caller` loads RCX, RDX
    and R8 from the pointer it is handed, never touches R9, leaves a 1 in the
    fifth-argument slot, and calls through a memory slot, the IAT shape, so
    the callee has no body to answer for its own arity.

    Pass 1 (`option stackarggap off`) reproduces the BUG: the call takes five
    arguments, the last two invented.
    Pass 2 (`option stackarggap on`) is the FIX: the call takes the three the
    disassembly passes.
-->
